INT. ABANDONED PLANETARIUM - NIGHT

A dead dome. Seats in tiers. Dust turns slowly in a flashlight beam.

BILLIE STROUD, 40s, detective, coat open, steps over a tipped chair. A projector lies on its side.

BILLIE
Peregrine Lowe.

From the dark of the control booth, a man leans into the light: PEREGRINE LOWE, 60s, tweed, handsome in a ruined way, a lantern on his knee.

PEREGRINE
Detective Stroud. You found it faster than I'd hoped.

BILLIE
The ticket stub in the victim's coat.

PEREGRINE
I thought you'd miss it.

BILLIE
You wanted me to find it.

PEREGRINE
Billie, I wanted you to look up.

Billie lifts the flashlight. Overhead, the dome is painted with constellations, hand-lettered. One star is circled in red.

BILLIE
That isn't Orion.

PEREGRINE
It's the only wrong star in the sky. That's how Marguerite signed her work.

BILLIE
She's been dead since 1998.

PEREGRINE
That's what I was told.

He turns off the lantern. The dome goes black. Then, soft, a hum, and the ceiling begins to glow, one star at a time.

BILLIE
Peregrine. What did you start?

PEREGRINE
Nothing. Watch.

Billie lowers the flashlight. The wrong star pulses. A door she hasn't seen opens in the dome's painted rim.

BILLIE
Who's behind that?

PEREGRINE
Billie. She has been waiting a long time to be asked.
